WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.240
"Fasting for Cancer:
What About Cachexia?"

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.680
In 1974, an influential paper
was published decrying

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.320
physician-induced malnutrition as
the skeleton in the hospital closet ---

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.600
the fact that many patients in
hospitals were malnourished,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.560
which the editorial board
of the journal of the AMA

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.560
described as shocking.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.360
"Even a single case
is one too many,"

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.640
yet still to this day
the issue persists.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.920
If anything, people with serious
illness would seem to need

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.040
even more nutrition,
not less, yet underfeeding

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.600
persists, involving as many as
50% of hospitalized patients.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.920
The ethical principle of justice
requires that every patient

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.560
be fed enough, given
that hospital malnutrition

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.240
has been associated
with increased

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.600
risk of disease and death,
but is it cause and effect?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.240
Does eating less
make you sicker,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.160
or does being sicker
just make you eat less?

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.240
You don't know until
you put it to the test.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.240
But would it be ethical to randomize
patients to remain starved?

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.760
I mean wouldn't nutritional
support obviously help?

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.960
It turns out, no. Not one,
but 22 randomized controlled

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.520
trials involving thousands
of malnourished patients

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.840
found that, sure,
you can plump them up;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.000
however, there seemed to be
little effect on clinical outcomes.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.040
In fact, sometimes it can
actually make things worse.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.240
Maybe your body is losing
your appetite on purpose.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.560
Ever since Hippocrates, fasting
has been offered as a treatment

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.760
for acute and chronic diseases,
based on the observation

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.960
that when people get sick, they
frequently lose their appetite,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.080
so maybe that's part of our body's
wisdom and we shouldn't force it?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.240
OK, but that was 2400 years ago.
What have we learned since?

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.680
Along with fever, decreased
food consumption is indeed

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.560
one of the most common signs
of infection, often regarded

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.080
as an undesirable
manifestation of sickness,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.000
but it's actually an active,
beneficial defense mechanism.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.600
Now obviously, chronic
under-nutrition can impair

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.800
our defenses, but data
suggest that in the short-term,

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.640
immune function can be enhanced
by lowering food intake.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.480
Some of the data are crazy,
like 95% alive versus 95% dead

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.120
after the same infection,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.320
but that was in mice
starved for 48 hours.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.560
Obviously, you can't randomize
people to a fatal infection,

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.600
but what they showed is that
the blood from starved mice was

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.800
nearly 8 times better at
killing off the invading bacteria

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.840
in a petri dish.
It dramatically boosted

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.440
the capacity of their white blood
cells to kill off the pathogens.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.720
Why can't we just test people
like that? Indeed, we can.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.280
Researchers fasted
people for two weeks

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.520
on an 80 calorie a day diet,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.480
and their white blood cells
showed the same kind of boost

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.640
in bacteria-killing activity,
a boost in antibody production,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.080
and natural killer cell activity
increased by an average of 24%.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.520
Now that's especially interesting
because our natural killer cells

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.040
don't just help clear infections,
but also kill cancer cells.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.640
In fact, that's how they measured
natural killer cell activity by pitting

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.800
them against K562 cells --- those are
tumor cells, human leukemia cells.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.000
So two weeks of fasting boosted
their bloodstream's ability

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.880
to kill off cancer cells by 24%.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.000
So fasting is said to improve
anticancer immunosurveillance,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.680
or more poetically

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.960
"stimulate the appetite of
the immune system for cancer. "

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.920
So why isn't fasting used more
to treat cancer? Until recently,

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.160
fasting therapy was not considered
to be a treatment option

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.040
in cancer, related to the fact that
a common therapeutic goal

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.920
in palliative cancer treatment
is to avoid weight loss

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.440
and to counteract the wasting
syndrome known as cachexia,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.680
which is the ultimate cause
of death in many cancer cases.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.040
Tumors are voracious,
rapidly expanding,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.120
and needing lots of
energy and protein,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.720
and so metabolically
reprograms our body

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.920
to start breaking down to feed it.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.920
It does this by triggering
inflammation throughout the body.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.320
It's not just that people
lose their appetite.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.640
The fundamental difference
between weight loss

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.640
observed in cancer cachexia
and that seen in simple starvation

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.400
is the lack of reversibility
with feeding alone.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.160
For example, here's the weight of
a cancer patient that started to drop.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.680
No wonder: they were only taking
in a few hundred calories a day.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.720
So in addition to giving them
about 100 grams of protein a day,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.320
they stuck a tube into a vein and
infused up to 4,000 calories a day.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.640
But it didn't matter.
They continued to lose weight.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.000
Therapeutic nutritional
interventions to correct or reverse

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.240
cachexia have met
with little success.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.320
The best treatment for
cancer cachexia therefore

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.440
is to treat the cause
and cure the cancer.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.720
In fact, maybe forcing extra
nutrition on cancer patients

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.320
could be playing right
into the tumor's hands.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.520
Like in pregnancy when the
fetus gets first dibs on nutrients

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.200
even at the mother's expense,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.120
the tumor may be first
in the feeding line.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.360
Maybe our loss of appetite
when we get cancer

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.600
is even a protective response.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:37.280
But in the 1960s, TPN was born ---
total parenteral nutrition ---

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.360
where people no longer had to eat;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.080
and you could infuse all the
nutrition people needed straight

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.720
into their veins, and the modern
era of nutrition support was born.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.000
It became widely accepted
and implemented,

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.160
growing into a
multibillion-dollar industry.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.160
So should it be routinely given to
malnourished cancer patients?

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.840
The answer is not as
obvious as one might think.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.800
When it was put to the test
in dozens of randomized trials,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.000
the results were both
disappointing and surprising.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.080
Parenteral nutrition didn't just
fail to provide any benefit

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.960
to these patients;
it caused harm.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.320
Not only did it appear to
provide zero survival benefit,

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.080
there was an increase in
complications and infections,

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.240
and a decrease in tumor
response to chemotherapy,

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.800
presumed to be due to all
those extra nutrients

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.560
stimulating tumor growth.
Similarly, oral nutritional

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.640
interventions in malnourished
patients with cancer,

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:40.480
like giving them bottles of Ensure,
found no survival advantage.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.760
Despite the lack of demonstrated
benefit, the knee jerk reaction

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.960
of many oncologists to the idea
of cancer patients fasting

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.400
is the concern they're not
eating enough already.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.800
But you don't know
until you put it to the test,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.864
which we'll explore next.

